  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 6NT. South has 11 top tricks (2 spades, 3 hearts, 2 diamonds, and 4 clubs). A 3-3 heart break is the only immediately evident source of a possible twelfth trick.\n\nWith proper play, South can succeed when hearts are 4-2 as long as the defender long in hearts also has 4 or more diamonds. An extra trick can be made with a simple squeeze.\n\nSimple squeezes mature with only 1 loser. As South has 2 losers he must duck a trick, rectifying the count. West's opening spade lead could be ducked, but if diamonds are 4-3, both opponents guard the suit and the squeeze will fail.\n\nSouth does better to win the opening lead and duck a round of diamonds. South wins whatever is played back, unblocks the diamonds, and runs the clubs.\n\nOn the last club East must surrender (the squeeze would work equally well against West). A heart discard will make North's fourth heart good. A diamond discard will create a diamond trick in South's hand.\n\nNot only did ducking a diamond trick serve to rectify the count, it also isolated the diamond guard in East's hand.",
